  "source_dossier": {
    "summary_short": "Vago and Silbersweig propose S-ART as a framework for mindfulness built around self-awareness, self-regulation, and self-transcendence. The review links meditation practice to biased self-processing, neurocognitive control, body regulation, prosociality, and a research agenda for testing specific mechanisms rather than treating mindfulness as a black box.",
    "summary_long": "Vago and Silbersweig's article is a theory-building review that tries to make mindfulness scientifically tractable without flattening it into one vague attentional skill. The central move is the S-ART framework: mindfulness training develops self-awareness, self-regulation, and self-transcendence, and these capacities reduce biased self-processing. The authors argue that suffering and psychopathology often involve distorted narrative self-schemas, affect-biased attention, and rumination. Mindfulness practices are therefore framed as methods for noticing and modifying the conditions that create these distortions. The review connects this psychological argument to a neurobiological model of self-processing. It distinguishes embodied, present-centered, and autobiographical forms of self-related processing, then proposes that mindfulness-based practice can modulate self-specifying and narrative self networks through integrative fronto-parietal control. The framework is broad: it includes intention and motivation, attention regulation, emotion regulation, extinction and reconsolidation, prosociality, non-attachment, and decentering. Empirical support is synthesized rather than newly generated. The article cites reports of brief training effects, physiological changes during meditation, immune and stress-reactivity findings, and network-level hypotheses. It also emphasizes limits. The authors warn that mindfulness research needs more precise operational definitions, objective measures of practice quality, longitudinal designs, dose-response evidence, and attention to adverse effects, especially in long-term or intensive practice contexts. The result is not a settled mechanistic proof, but a detailed map for decomposing mindfulness into testable components and for connecting contemplative practice to self-processing, clinical change, and brain-body regulation.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Central Argument",
        "body": "The article's main argument is that mindfulness is best understood as training in self-awareness, self-regulation, and self-transcendence rather than as one generic mental state. This reframing lets the authors connect meditation practice to biased self-processing, clinical symptoms, and prosocial change without relying on a single overloaded definition of mindfulness.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "0",
          "1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Model of Suffering",
        "body": "The paper treats suffering and psychopathology as partly maintained by distorted narrative self-processing. Habitual negative beliefs, affect-biased attention, rumination, and symptoms reinforce one another until a self-schema becomes reified. Mindfulness matters because it is proposed to expose and loosen this loop rather than merely suppress symptoms.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Neurocognitive Architecture",
        "body": "S-ART separates self-processing into embodied enactive, present phenomenological, and autobiographical narrative modes. That taxonomy supports the claim that mindfulness may alter relationships among self-specifying and narrative systems through fronto-parietal control, while preserving the article's caution that this is a simplified scaffold rather than a complete account of selfhood.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
